$ ss -lnu | grep -E ':(123|323) '
UNCONN 0      0          127.0.0.1:323       0.0.0.0:*          
UNCONN 0      0              [::1]:323          [::]:*          

$ systemctl is-active chrony
active

$ systemctl start lab-ntp-server


========== 第4章: rsyslog は既定で書くたびの同期をしない / リモート転送の @ と @@ ==========

$ man 5 rsyslog.conf 2>/dev/null | grep -A5 'files are not synced'
       By default, files are not synced after each write. To enable syncing of
       log files globally, use either the "$ActionFileEnableSync" directive or
       the  "sync"  parameter to omfile. Enabling this option degrades perfor‐
       mance and it is advised not to enable syncing unless you know what  you
       are  doing.   To selectively disable syncing for certain files, you may
       prefix the file path with a minus sign ("-").

$ grep -rn -i 'sync' /etc/rsyslog.conf /etc/rsyslog.d/; echo "exit=$?"
exit=1

$ man 5 rsyslog.conf 2>/dev/null | grep -A3 'To  *forward messages to another host via UDP'
       To  forward messages to another host via UDP, prepend the hostname with
       the at sign ("@").  To forward it via plain tcp, prepend two  at  signs
       ("@@").  To forward via RELP, prepend the string ":omrelp:" in front of
       the hostname.

$ man 5 rsyslog.conf 2>/dev/null | grep -B2 -A1 'destination port defaults to 514'

       In the example above, messages are forwarded via  UDP  to  the  machine
       192.168.0.1, the destination port defaults to 514. Due to the nature of
       UDP,  you  will  probably lose some messages in transit.  If you expect

$ man 1 logger 2>/dev/null | grep -A2 -E '^ +-T, --tcp'
       -T, --tcp
           Use stream (TCP) only. By default the connection is tried to the
           syslog-conn port defined in /etc/services, which is often 601.


========== 第5章: 99- で始まる名前では ForwardToSyslog=no が効かない（名前順で後の syslog.conf が勝つ） ==========

$ mkdir -p /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d

$ printf '[Journal]\nForwardToSyslog=no\n' > /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-noforward.conf

$ systemctl restart systemd-journald

$ logger -p user.info "99番で止めたつもり"

$ grep -c "99番で止めたつもり" /var/log/syslog
1

$ systemd-analyze cat-config systemd/journald.conf | grep -E '^# /|^ForwardToSyslog'
# /etc/systemd/journald.conf
# /etc/ if the original file is shipped in /usr/), or by creating "drop-ins" in
# /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-noforward.conf
ForwardToSyslog=no
# /usr/lib/systemd/journald.conf.d/syslog.conf
ForwardToSyslog=yes

$ rm /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-noforward.conf

$ systemctl restart systemd-journald


========== 第5章: systemd-cat が付けられるラベル（priority だけ。facility は無い） ==========

$ systemd-cat --help
systemd-cat [OPTIONS...] COMMAND ...

Execute process with stdout/stderr connected to the journal.

  -h --help                      Show this help
     --version                   Show package version
  -t --identifier=STRING         Set syslog identifier
  -p --priority=PRIORITY         Set priority value (0..7)
     --stderr-priority=PRIORITY  Set priority value (0..7) used for stderr
     --level-prefix=BOOL         Control whether level prefix shall be parsed

See the systemd-cat(1) man page for details.


